      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Before the Long Walk, the area that is now Sheep Springs hosted at least eleven Navajo bands, including the group led by Narbona.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/sheep_springs_new_mexico",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Sheep Springs thrives on traditional rug weaving and sand painting, while also hosting a chapter house, post office, and weekend flea market.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/sheep_springs_new_mexico",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The rugged terrain around Sheep Springs includes Tsé bizhdílnihí, a fragment of Monster Rock tied to Navajo myths of Twin Warriors and a protective snake spirit.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/sheep_springs_new_mexico",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Sheep Springs Chapter evolved from informal 1920s meetings to a certified 1965 chapter house, marking a shift from verbal line‑ups to secret ballots every four years.",
